 **Price tiers.** A task item's `model` may also be `$`, `$$`, `$$$` or `$$$$` (task calls only; eval calls need an exact selector). The tiers are quarters of the blended price (input plus output per million tokens) across every loaded chat model, so a tier means the same thing whichever providers you are signed in to. Models with no price (free tiers, subscriptions, local models) take no tier. A tier resolves to the configured role in that tier, or in the nearest tier when there is none (the cheaper side wins a tie), and from there it is approved, dispatched and revived exactly like that role. Within a tier the most used role wins. Usage is the share of the last 90 days of requests in `stats.db` (read-only, from the hourly rollup), weighted by how specific the match is: the exact `provider/model-id` counts 0.6, the same model family 0.3 and the same provider 0.1. With no history the order falls back to the role name. The task description shows each configured role's tier, tells agents to use the cheapest tier that does the work reliably, and calls `$$$$` very rare. The judge that picks a role for a call without `model` is shown the same tiers and usage shares.
 
+### Changing a running subagent's model
+
+`write proc://<agentId>/model` with a configured role (`@haiku`), a price tier (`$` to `$$$$`, resolved exactly as for a task `model`) or one exact `provider/model-id[:level]` as `content` switches a running subagent that the caller spawned (the task description tells the agent to use it when the user names a model or tier for a running agent, for example "use haiku for RefactorWork"). The id is the agent's name or the id of the job running it. The call never aborts or restarts the agent: it finishes the request in flight on its current model, and its next model request uses the new one, with its context, tools and turn intact. The change is session-only (a `temporary` model change in the agent's transcript, plus a notice in its view when a queued switch lands) and is not persisted to settings or roles. An agent between requests switches immediately; a switch queued while a request is in flight is applied at the next request boundary and the newest one wins. A per-call worker's saved identity follows the switch, so a revival restores it on the model it last ran on.
+
+The model-facing route takes the approval a task `model` does: `task.perCallModel` must be `on` (project settings can only turn it off), the selector is one configured role or one exact selector, and the model must be an available chat model the operator's configuration grants, within `task.perCallModelAllow` and able to run at or below `task.maxEffort`. Grants are read from the agent's own definition and the caller's live model as for a spawn. It is refused, and nothing changes, when the agent was not spawned by the caller, does not exist, has finished (anything but `running`), or the model is not approved or unavailable. Retry fallback is unchanged: a per-call worker never falls back, and any other agent keeps its `subagent:<id>` chain. The user switching from the subagent's own view (`/switch`, the model picker) is the operator and is not gated; the view applies the same deferred switch ([Agent Hub](../agent-hub.md)), and a per-call worker's dispatch gate admits the models the user chose.
+
 Runtime stays permissive: the flat form is accepted even while `task.batch` is on (internal callers such as the commit flow's `analyze_files`, and stale transcripts). The model only ever sees one shape. Settled results report the requested and actual model; extension routing, auth fallback, and runtime retry fallback can switch the actual model under the existing policies.
 
 Batch calls reject a top-level `agent` rather than silently discarding a specialist selector and spawning the default agent. Put `agent` on each `tasks[]` item when selecting `reviewer`, `scout`, or a custom agent; the agent name is not inferred from the task text.
